          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "canonical_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Salivary mucoceles (sialoceles) are nonepitheliallined cavities filled with saliva that has leaked from a damaged salivary gland or duct; they are surrounded by granulation tissue that forms secondary to inflammation caused by the free saliva.",
              "pdf_page": 1296,
              "printed_page": 1214,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "There are four major pairs of salivary glands: parotid, mandibular, sublingual, and zygomatic. Smaller salivary glands are located in the soft palate, lips, cheeks, and tongue.",
              "pdf_page": 1296,
              "printed_page": 1214,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Types of mucoceles are listed in Table 1. The most common type occurs with injury to the sublingual gland or its duct.",
              "pdf_page": 1296,
              "printed_page": 1214,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
